# aegis-base-php — the THIRD base image aegis OWNS: alpine + the
# distribution's PHP 8.4 and nginx, listening on 8080, running as UID
# 65532. It is what an organization's `http` services written in PHP put
# in their FROM.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL
# ────────────────────
# Until today this installation could not run PHP. Not "it was not
# convenient": the require-aegis-signature ClusterPolicy demands our own
# signature for every Pod in a tenant namespace, so a public php image
# cannot be admitted, and there was no php in mirror-images/ either. The
# first contract that asks for Laravel is the one that makes this file
# necessary.
#
# It is an OWNED base and not a mirrored third party, and that choice is
# the whole lesson nginx/Containerfile tells in full: a mirror pins
# somebody else's bytes and CANNOT PATCH THEM. On 2026-08-26 that cost
# every static front several days of blocked deploys over an openssl the
# fronts never call, because upstream had not rebuilt its image. An
# owned base is `alpine + apk upgrade + apk add`: the distribution's
# packages with the distribution's patch cadence, rebuilt by the
# base-images job the morning image-watch finds a fixable CVE, and
# propagated by digest to every consumer.
#
# TWO PROCESSES IN ONE CONTAINER, AND IT IS THE FIRST TIME HERE
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# php-fpm does not speak HTTP; it speaks FastCGI. Something has to
# terminate HTTP on 8080, and the options were three:
#   · nginx + php-fpm in this one container  ← this file
#   · two containers in the Pod: nginx would need the app's public/,
#     which lives in the consumer's image, so an initContainer would
#     have to copy it across. More pieces, more to get wrong;
#   · FrankenPHP, one binary that speaks HTTP directly. Ruled OUT by
#     the base-images job itself, which requires the FROM to name
#     alpine:<major>.<minor> — so it could only enter as a mirrored
#     third party, i.e. the exact disease described above.
# So: two processes, and the honest cost of that is that a supervisor
# has to exist and has to be RIGHT. entrypoint.sh dies when either of
# them dies, because a container that stays Running while nginx serves
# 502 is the silent failure this platform keeps paying for. Nothing
# asserts that at build time — it is verified by killing each process in
# the live pod and watching the restart.
#
# WHAT A CONSUMER MAY ASSUME (the contract, asserted by the job before
# the image is pushed):
#   - it listens on 8080 (a tenant's NetworkPolicy admits only
#     edge → 8080; a server on any other port starts fine and NEVER
#     receives traffic — the silent failure);
#   - it runs as UID/GID 65532, non-root (tenant namespaces are PSS
#     `restricted`: root is rejected at admission and a non-numeric
#     USER fails runAsNonRoot because the kubelet cannot prove it);
#   - /app exists, is owned by 65532, and is the WORKDIR. nginx serves
#     /app/public and passes .php to php-fpm on 127.0.0.1:9000;
#   - `php` is on the PATH (see the symlink below — it is NOT there by
#     default and that is a trap);
#   - it writes NOTHING outside /tmp. The pid, every nginx temp path
#     and the php session path live there, because tenant pods run with
#     a read-only root filesystem.
#
# WHY UID 65532 AND NOT nginx's 101. This base runs an application, not
# a web server that happens to have files: the number that matters is
# the one the consumer's `COPY --chown` uses, and every repo in this
# installation already carries 65532 (it is aegis-base-node's, which is
# distroless's `nonroot`). A PHP repo then looks like every other repo.
# The cost is that nginx does not run as its package's own user, so
# everything it touches is chowned to 65532 below.
#
# WHAT IS NOT HERE, ON PURPOSE
#   - composer. Production runs PHP; the consumer installs its vendor/
#     in the CI agent (aegis-ci-php) and copies it in. A dependency
#     manager inside a tenant pod has nothing to do there — the same
#     criterion that keeps npm out of aegis-base-node.
#   - php84-intl, AND THIS ONE IS A LESSON PAID FOR ELSEWHERE. Alpine's
#     intl links against icu-libs, which brings `icu-data-en`: REDUCED
#     locale data. That is failure nº 2 of the field notes exactly —
#     a container whose ICU silently falls back to `en` and formats
#     dates in an order the code did not expect. Without intl the
#     temptation does not exist: dates are formatted with explicit
#     format strings and an explicit timezone, which is locale
#     independent, and PHP carries its own timezone database so no
#     tzdata package is needed either.
#   - php84-zip. composer wants it, and composer is not here.

# The FROM is the SAME public digest nginx/Containerfile and
# node/Containerfile pin, for the reasons told there in full: pulled
# from the PUBLIC registry, by DIGEST, and not from the internal mirror
# (whose blocking scan would refuse the stale alpine underneath, so this
# base could never be built from it). The alpine's own packages never
# run: `apk upgrade` replaces them on the next line, and what runs is
# the RESULT, which is scanned with NO exceptions and signed by the job.
# The minor here is the single source of the image's tag: the job
# derives `<minor>-<build>` from this line.
# De acá sale el nginx. Es la base que esta plataforma ya construye, escanea y
# firma —la que sirve los fronts estáticos—, así que no agrega ningún eslabón
# nuevo a la cadena de suministro: es un eslabón que ya estaba, reutilizado.
FROM registry.registry-system.svc.cluster.local:5000/aegis-base-nginx:3.22-000009@sha256:4329e17592baeeb173fdf06802cdde953a5ed8204bc20435e0c8ddd69d8e50c6 AS nginxbin

FROM public.ecr.aws/docker/library/alpine:3.22@sha256:14358309a308569c32bdc37e2e0e9694be33a9d99e68afb0f5ff33cc1f695dce

# ORDER MATTERS, and every step below has a reason:
#
# 1. uid/gid 65532 first, by number, under the name distroless gave
#    them: `nonroot`. The nginx package creates a user of its own at the
#    first free system uid; creating ours first keeps the two from
#    racing for a number, and the assertion below breaks the BUILD, with
#    this line in the log, if that assumption is ever wrong — instead of
#    a tenant pod failing with EACCES on a file every `--chown=65532`
#    had promised it.
# 2. `apk upgrade` BEFORE `apk add`. The alpine above is frozen by
#    digest: its packages are whatever they were the day it was built
#    upstream. The upgrade is where today's patched packages come in,
#    and it is the line image-watch rebuilds this image to run.
# 3. The extension list is Laravel's documented requirement and nothing
#    more, plus pdo_pgsql because every organization's database here is
#    postgres, and opcache because the code in an image never changes
#    (see conf.d/zz-aegis.ini, which turns timestamp validation off).
#    ctype/fileinfo/dom/xml/simplexml/xmlwriter/tokenizer/mbstring/
#    openssl/session/phar/iconv/curl/sodium: Laravel boots without any
#    one of them only to die later, in a request, in a stack trace that
#    names a class and not a package. `filter` and `hash` are compiled
#    into the core in PHP 8 and have no package of their own.
#
#    NO php84-opcache, Y ES UN HALLAZGO MEDIDO. Enlaza contra libgcc_s.so.1
#    y no la declara; agregar el paquete `libgcc` no alcanza, porque en la
#    imagen publicada ese archivo NO EXISTE aunque apk diga que el paquete
#    está — ni siquiera alcanza crearlo a mano. Y el build pasaba igual,
#    porque el assert corre DENTRO de kaniko, donde el archivo sí está: un
#    assert que corre dentro del builder no es un assert sobre la imagen.
#    Lo que quedaba era una advertencia por stderr en cada invocación de
#    php, que es la forma exacta en que un problema se vuelve invisible.
#    Es una optimización, no una funcionalidad. Ver ci-images/php.
# 4. THE SYMLINK, and it is the trap of this whole file. The package
#    installs /usr/bin/php84 and PROVIDES the virtual name `php` — but
#    provides is apk's dependency vocabulary, not a file. There is no
#    /usr/bin/php. Every artisan invocation, every composer script and
#    every shebang in vendor/bin says `php`. Without this line the image
#    builds clean, passes the contract, passes the scan, gets signed,
#    deploys — and the first `php artisan` in an entrypoint says
#    "not found".
# 5. Logs to stdout/stderr through symlinks, as the official images do:
#    the pod's log is what Vector reads; a file in the container is read
#    by nobody.
# 6. Everything nginx and php touch at runtime is chowned to 65532. The
#    temp paths are NOT here — they live under /tmp (see nginx.conf),
#    because tenant pods run with a read-only root filesystem.
RUN addgroup -g 65532 -S nonroot \
 && adduser -u 65532 -D -S -H -h /nonexistent -s /sbin/nologin -G nonroot -g nonroot nonroot \
 && apk upgrade --no-cache \
 && apk add --no-cache \
      php84 php84-fpm php84-common \
      php84-ctype php84-curl php84-dom php84-fileinfo php84-iconv \
      php84-mbstring php84-openssl php84-pdo php84-pdo_pgsql \
      php84-phar php84-session php84-simplexml php84-sodium \
      php84-tokenizer php84-xml php84-xmlwriter \
 && [ "$(id -u nonroot)" = 65532 ] && [ "$(id -g nonroot)" = 65532 ] \
 && ln -sf /usr/bin/php84 /usr/bin/php \
 && mkdir -p /app/public \
 && chown -R 65532:65532 /app

# NGINX EN SU PROPIO `RUN`, Y ESTÁ MEDIDO.
#
# Instalado junto a los veinte paquetes de php en un solo `apk add`, el
# paquete se instalaba («(2/39) Installing nginx»), `nginx -t` pasaba, y en la
# imagen publicada NO QUEDABA NI UNO de sus archivos: ni /usr/sbin/nginx ni
# nada de /etc/nginx. El pod arrancaba, php-fpm levantaba, y el entrypoint
# moría con «nginx: not found». Comprobado recorriendo `apk info -L` de cada
# paquete instalado contra el sistema de archivos real de un pod: los ÚNICOS
# archivos ausentes eran los de nginx; los de php84, en el mismo directorio y
# del mismo `apk add`, estaban todos.
#
# Su propia capa lo arregla. Y la lección es más grande que el arreglo, porque
# es la segunda vez en este mismo archivo:
#
#   UN ASSERT QUE CORRE DENTRO DE KANIKO NO ES UN ASSERT SOBRE LA IMAGEN.
#
# `nginx -t` decía que sí sobre una imagen que no tenía nginx. Lo único que lo
# vio fue arrancar un pod. Por eso la verificación de esta base incluye correr
# la imagen, y no sólo construirla.
# NGINX NO SE INSTALA ACÁ: SE COPIA DE aegis-base-nginx.
#
# Cuatro intentos con apk y los cuatro produjeron una imagen SIN un solo
# archivo del paquete nginx —ni /usr/sbin/nginx ni nada de /etc/nginx—,
# mientras los veinte paquetes de php, del mismo `apk add` y en los mismos
# directorios, llegaban completos. Se probó: instalarlo junto a php, en su
# propia capa, creando antes el usuario que su script de pre-install crea, y
# con `--no-scripts`. Las cuatro veces el build pasó —incluido un `nginx -t`
# que valida la configuración— y la imagen publicada no tenía nginx. Medido
# recorriendo `apk info -L` de cada paquete instalado contra el sistema de
# archivos de un pod.
#
#   UN ASSERT QUE CORRE DENTRO DE KANIKO NO ES UN ASSERT SOBRE LA IMAGEN.
#   `nginx -t` dijo que sí cuatro veces sobre imágenes que no tenían nginx.
#   Lo único que lo vio fue arrancar un pod.
#
# Así que el binario viene de donde ya está y ya funciona: aegis-base-nginx,
# la base que esta misma plataforma construye, escanea y firma, y sobre la que
# corren los fronts estáticos. Y eso no es un parche — es mejor: en esta
# instalación hay UN solo nginx, construido una vez, y el día que image-watch
# encuentre una CVE en él se reconstruye en un lugar y llega a los dos.
#
# El `COPY --from` de una imagen del registro interno es un camino distinto del
# de apk, y ya está probado en este archivo: todo lo que se copia llega.
#
# LO QUE HAY QUE COPIAR, y por qué cada cosa:
#   /usr/sbin/nginx     el binario;
#   /etc/nginx          mime.types, fastcgi_params y compañía. Sin fastcgi_params
#                       el `include` de default.conf falla y nginx no arranca;
#   /usr/lib/nginx      los módulos que el binario espera encontrar.
# Las bibliotecas contra las que enlaza —pcre2, openssl, zlib— ya están acá:
# las trae php. Que eso sea cierto lo prueba el `nginx -v` de abajo... dentro
# del builder, así que la prueba de verdad es arrancar la imagen.
COPY --from=nginxbin /usr/sbin/nginx /usr/sbin/nginx
COPY --from=nginxbin /etc/nginx /etc/nginx
COPY --from=nginxbin /usr/lib/nginx /usr/lib/nginx

RUN addgroup -g 101 -S nginx \
 && adduser -u 101 -D -S -H -h /var/cache/nginx -s /sbin/nologin -G nginx -g nginx nginx \
 && test -x /usr/sbin/nginx \
 && mkdir -p /etc/nginx/conf.d /var/lib/nginx /var/log/nginx /run/nginx \
 && ln -sf /dev/stdout /var/log/nginx/access.log \
 && ln -sf /dev/stderr /var/log/nginx/error.log \
 && chown -R 65532:65532 /etc/nginx/conf.d /var/lib/nginx /var/log/nginx /run/nginx

# OUR OWN nginx.conf, for the same reason aegis-base-nginx has one:
# alpine's stock file includes conf.d in the ROOT context, where a
# `server{}` is "not allowed here" and nginx refuses to start.
COPY nginx.conf /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
COPY default.conf /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf
COPY php-fpm.conf /etc/php84/php-fpm.conf
COPY zz-aegis.ini /etc/php84/conf.d/zz-aegis.ini
COPY entrypoint.sh /usr/local/bin/aegis-php-entrypoint

# THE BUILD-TIME CONTRACT, sibling of nginx's `nginx -t` and node's
# `require("http")`. Every line here is a fact a consumer's pod depends
# on, checked where the failure is cheap. If a future package drops an
# extension, renames a binary or ships a config this layout cannot
# parse, the build dies HERE, with this line in the log — not six weeks
# later in somebody's pod, in a stack trace that names a class.
#
# The extension check is a LOOP over the list rather than a grep per
# line so that the missing one is NAMED. `php -m` and not `php84 -m`:
# it exercises the symlink from step 4 at the same time.
RUN set -eux; \
    php -v; \
    php-fpm84 --version; \
    for ext in ctype curl dom fileinfo iconv mbstring openssl pdo pdo_pgsql \
               Phar session SimpleXML sodium tokenizer xml xmlwriter; do \
      php -m | grep -qix "$ext" || { echo "MISSING PHP EXTENSION: $ext"; php -m; exit 1; }; \
    done; \
    php -r 'new DateTimeImmutable("now", new DateTimeZone("America/Santiago"));'; \
    php -r 'if (class_exists("IntlDateFormatter")) { fwrite(STDERR, "intl is installed: see the note about icu-data-en at the top of this file\n"); exit(1); }'; \
    if php -r 'exit(0);' 2>&1 | grep -q 'Failed loading'; then \
      echo "PHP AVISA QUE UNA EXTENSIÓN NO CARGA:"; php -r 'exit(0);'; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    sh -n /usr/local/bin/aegis-php-entrypoint

# The two servers, each run once at build time, ON THEIR OWN LINES.
# The shape is not cosmetic: it is what aegis-base-nginx does and what
# check 138 reads — the rule is "if the image installs a server, it RUNS
# it once at build time", and a `nginx -t;` buried in a longer chain
# does not answer it. nginx proves that nginx.conf includes conf.d in
# the http context (a `server{}` in the wrong context fails HERE instead
# of in the consumer's pod); php-fpm proves the pool file above parses.
RUN nginx -t

# EL CAMBIO DE USUARIO VA ANTES DEL TEST DE php-fpm, Y ESO ES EL TEST.
#
# php-fpm arrancado como root EXIGE `user`/`group` en el pool y aborta sin
# ellos; arrancado como cualquier otro uid los ignora y avisa si están.
# O sea: la configuración correcta para root es la incorrecta para este
# contenedor y viceversa. Build base-images#2 lo cobró — `php-fpm84 -t`
# corría como root y murió con «failed to post process the
# configuration», por un pool que en producción está bien.
#
# Así que el test corre con el uid que la imagen va a usar de verdad, que
# además es lo único que prueba algo: validar la configuración como un
# usuario que nunca va a ejecutarla no es validarla.
USER 65532:65532

# stderr capturado y volcado a mano si falla. kaniko escribe sus propios
# errores en el mismo descriptor y en #2 se comió la línea que decía el
# motivo, dejando media palabra («…cess the configuration») y una hora de
# adivinanzas. Un mensaje de error que se pierde es un mensaje de error
# que no existe.
RUN php-fpm84 -t 2>/tmp/fpm-test.log \
 || { echo '=== php-fpm84 -t falló; esto dijo: ==='; cat /tmp/fpm-test.log; exit 1; }

WORKDIR /app
EXPOSE 8080
# SIGQUIT is nginx's graceful stop, and entrypoint.sh forwards it to
# both children: in-flight requests finish, then everything exits.
STOPSIGNAL SIGQUIT
# THE INTERPRETER IS NAMED EXPLICITLY, and the entrypoint file is NOT
# executable. sh reads the file — mode 0644, world readable — and becomes
# PID 1 exactly as it would have through a shebang, so nothing is lost.
#
# What is gained is that this image cannot break because the file
# arrived without its mode. That is not hypothetical here: check 134
# forbids executables in the artifact, so entrypoint.sh is 0644 in the
# repository by rule, and an image that needed `chmod +x` at build time
# would be one RUN away from the class of failure that ci-images#2, #3
# and #4 spent three builds pinning down (ci-images/php/Containerfile
# tells that story). Not needing the bit at all is shorter than needing
# it and getting it right.
ENTRYPOINT ["/bin/sh", "/usr/local/bin/aegis-php-entrypoint"]
